ged line. With the line out, Bus_29 is at **0.931 p.u. at 18:00**, below the **0.950 p.u.** limit. At 15:00, under the same outage, it was **0.952 p.u.**\u2014technically within limits, but with only **0.002 p.u.** of margin.\n\nThe main difference is higher demand near the end of the line: demand at Bus_28 and Bus_29 rose by **2.27 MW** in total, accounting for about **78%** of the voltage drop between the two moments. Demand elsewhere also increased substantially and contributed about **22%**. Changes in generator output had essentially no effect on this voltage change.\n\nSo the contingency is close to the limit at 15:00 and crosses it at 18:00 as demand rises. The outaged line itself reports 0% loading because it is out of service; the security issue is the resulting **Bus_29 undervoltage**.\n\n\ud83d\udccb Data source: measurements. Load reactive demand was derived using the default power factor of 0.95.\n\n**Reading check.** CONDUCTOR re-ran the studies under readings the question left open, and the conclusion changes: with loads at power factor 1.00 instead of 0.95 (the default), the investigation finds no change to explain. Say which reading you meant.", "reading_check": {"headline": "Depends on the reading: the conclusion changes under 1 of 4 other readings.", "outcomes": [{"tool": "investigate", "reading": "data_source", "label": "the forecast for 2000-01-06 18:00:00", "assumed": "the measurements", "overrides": {"data_source": "forecasts", "timestamp": "2000-01-06 18:00:00"}, "status": "shifts", "before": "main cause Demand near Bus_29 (2 buses) (78 % of the change)", "after": "main cause Demand (21 buses) (100 % of the change)", "seconds": 0.8}, {"tool": "investigate", "reading": "load_power_factor", "label": "loads at power factor 0.90", "assumed": "0.95 (the default)", "overrides": {"load_power_factor": 0.9}, "status": "holds", "before": "main cause Demand near Bus_29 (2 buses) (78 % of the change)", "after": "main cause Demand near Bus_29 (2 buses) (7